Subject: [PATCH 1/7] i686: delete superseded global_store_forwarding, restore
 its soundness tests

Enabling i686 testing (libc6-dev-i386 + gcc-multilib; i386 ELF runs natively,
no QEMU) lifted the corpus from 292 to 792 TUs and made execution-based
validation possible. Three results follow.

1. `global_store_forwarding` is deleted. It shipped disabled behind
   `#[expect(dead_code)]` with a TODO blaming "FP load/store forwarding
   patterns" for 21 regressions in matrix/FP tests. That diagnosis is wrong:
   the parsers only ever accepted movl/movw/movb, so no FP value was ever
   forwarded. The defect was width-blind invalidation -- a write cleared one
   byte-offset entry, so `fstpl -8(%ebp)` (8 bytes, -8..-1) left a tracked
   `movl %eax,-4(%ebp)` alive and the next `movl -4(%ebp),%ecx` forwarded a
   stale %eax instead of the double's high half. Three more defects: the write
   check was gated on s.contains("(%ebp)") so %esp writes were invisible; the
   map was indexed -off-1 under off<0 while %esp offsets are biased positive by
   ESP_SLOT_BIAS, rejecting all 100,442 %esp operands; and %esp movement was
   never fenced. `forward_slot_loads` already does this transformation, is
   enabled, and is sound in all four places, so the dead pass is removed with
   the adjudication recorded in-file rather than fixed into a duplicate.

2. Its tests were deleted along with it when it was disabled, leaving the
   failure mode with no coverage. Five tests restore it against the live pass:
   a positive control (so the negatives cannot pass vacuously), the exact
   wide-x87-store mechanism, the 16-byte SSE window-kill constant, %esp
   adjustment fencing, and push/pop fencing -- the last also pinning the
   deliberate conservatism of fencing %ebp slots too, which narrowing would
   need a frame layout a text peephole does not have, for 2,085 of 102,527
   operands.

3. The evidence README's claim that extending parse_frame_slot to %esp/%ebp is
   a two-line i686 follow-up is retracted in place: lccc-i686 uses a separate
   13,491-line peephole, and the change was applied and measured as
   byte-identical across all 792 TUs.

New: FOLLOWUP-2026-09-12-i686-x87-gp-pair-staging.md records the measured
i686 gap (geomean 1.264x slower than gcc -m32; nbody 16.05x, matmul 13.80x),
isolates the mechanism (FP values staged through GP register pairs into slots
instead of x87 memory operands; 1,317 foldable sites, ~5,268 instructions,
~30% of nbody), specifies the fix with four soundness conditions and a
validation plan, and closes five negative results so they are not retried.
AUDIT-2026-09-12-S17-redteam.md adjudicates the merged S15/S16 claims and
records three claims this session made and then disproved with its own
instrumentation.

Output is byte-identical on 791 i686 + 804 x86-64 corpus TUs (dead-code
removal plus tests). 2484 lib tests pass, clippy clean, ci_local.sh --fast
25/0/3 green.
