From 83637e9964084baf77c158494bbfe4d4862ef614 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:54:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] i686: fold x87 GP-pair F64 staging into a direct memory
 operand

The i686 backend lowers an F64 load from a computed address exactly like an
I64/U64 load (memory.rs lumps IrType::F64 in with I64 | U64 | D64 when it
chooses a location), so a double read through a pointer is materialised as a GP
register pair, homed to a stack slot, and only then handed to the x87 unit:

    movl 24(%esi), %eax       movl 24(%esi)
    movl 28(%esi), %edx   ->  fldl 24(%esi)
    movl %eax, 68(%esp)
    movl %edx, 72(%esp)
    fldl 68(%esp)

Five instructions and a stack round-trip where GAS accepts one. GCC -m32 emits
the one-instruction form, which is why matmul's inner loop is 7 instructions
against lccc's ~24 and why the runtime gap reaches 13.8x (matmul) and 16.1x
(nbody).

fold_x87_gp_pair_staging rewrites the fldl in place and nops the four staging
lines, so the x87 push stays at the same program point and stack depth, operand
order and every fmulp/faddp pairing are untouched.

Soundness is seven conditions, each derived from a measured failure of a weaker
version; the load-bearing one is that the whole function never forms a frame
address in a register (lea/mov of %esp, or %ebp when it is a frame pointer, or a
copy of either). That is what makes the purely textual, %esp-relative slot
tracking complete: with no aliasing pointer in existence, the only operand that
can name the staged bytes is D(%esp), so every read and write of them is visible
to a displacement comparison. The dead-store post-condition is a forward
reachability search over the CFG rather than a linear scan, because the hot
sites are inside loops and a linear scan has to give up at the back edge; it
tracks %esp shifts (subl/addl/push/pop renumber every slot) and accepts a wide
x87 store (fstl/fstpl retire both halves at once, which is nbody's shape).

Indexed (SIB) sources fold too, and that is what makes the loop-carried shape
foldable at all: the two staging loads must have identical addressing text with
displacements exactly 4 apart, and EVERY register the address is computed from -
base and index - must stay unchanged across the window, so (%esi,%ecx,8) folds
while `addl $1, %edi` in the window refuses it. movl_operands therefore splits
on the first comma AT PAREN DEPTH ZERO; splitting on the first comma outright
parsed `movl (%ebx,%edi,8), %eax` as ("(%ebx", "%edi,8), %eax") and silently
rejected every SIB-addressed site. No corpus measurement can see a hole like
that - 0 of 792 TUs change either way, because the corpus sites that survive the
other conditions are base-only - and a unit test with a positive control found
it in one run.

Measured on the 792-TU i686 corpus against the same binary with the kill switch
CCC_NO_X87_PAIR_FOLD set: -948 instructions (-0.349%), 38 TUs improved, none
worse, 711 fewer frame-slot references; with the switch set the corpus
reproduces the recorded pre-fold baseline exactly (271,585 instructions /
102,701 slot references), which is the proof that the pass is inert when
disabled rather than merely quiet. nbody -128 instructions (1,485 -> 1,357) and
-7.5..-8.0% runtime, reproduced by two independent runs of the same binary (min
of 9: 7665.6ms -> 7052.8ms; min of 7: 7587.4ms -> 7019.5ms; oracle gap 16.07x
-> 14.49x). matmul -1.5% and vector_remainder (1.3ms total) are inside the noise
floor and are reported as uninformative, not as wins.

Correctness: all 38 changed TUs assemble with gcc -m32 -c (0 assembly failures,
including the rewritten SIB operands); the 33 that link and run are
byte-identical with the fold on and off, and 29 of those match gcc -m32 -O2
output exactly - the 4 that differ do so in both lccc arms identically, i.e.
pre-existing. A 250-program differential fuzz over the fold's domain (11
generators, 6 of them adversaries: address-taken local, call in the window,
index register rewritten, slot overwritten before consumption, union punning,
volatile) built and ran 250/250, fired the fold on 103, reported 0 off!=on
differences, fired 0 times on every adversary, and folded the loop-indexed
generator 23/23 correctly. Inline asm was adversarially compiled in three shapes
- the slot read by C code afterwards, the GP pair still live inside the asm, and
a numeric local label with a backward branch straddling the staging - and all
three are byte-identical between arms and equal to gcc.

Twelve in-tree unit tests drive the pass directly (the idiom forward_slot_loads
uses, because later pipeline passes rewrite the same text and would mask which
pass produced the shape under test). Four are positive: the basic 5->1 rewrite,
one wide x87 store retiring the pair (nbody's fstl shape), across a loop back
edge, and across an %esp shift. Eight are negative and each carries its own
positive control - the same function minus the offending line, asserted to fold -
so a refusal cannot pass for the wrong reason: slot re-read, base rewritten,
index rewritten, frame address formed, unmodelled write in the window, call in
the window, and %esp exchanged both before and after the load. cargo test --lib:
2,497 passed, 0 failed. ci_local.sh --fast: 25 passed, 0 failed, 3 skipped.
clippy --all-targets -D warnings: clean.

This is an interim mitigation and it is a partial one: 948 of the ~5,268
instructions the opportunity census identified, 237 of the 775 load-side sites.
The store-side mirror (542 sites, ~2,168 instructions, 62 of them in
vectorize_matmul_n16) is NOT implemented - it is the larger remaining half, and
the machinery here is directly reusable for it. The root fix is an x87 location
model in the backend (FOLLOWUP-2026-09-12-i686-x87-gp-pair-staging.md section
3.1); if that is ever built this pass should be deleted, not kept, and the kill
switch makes that a one-line, fully measurable change.
